    "question": {
      ":question/id": "bb950e07-e2a4-577b-b8c0-5d6b5db7b91f",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(10+7x)^{5}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{5},\\,g=10+7x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=5g^{4},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=7.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=5g^{4}\\cdot (7) \\\\  &=35(10+7x)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "9faa58e6-c50e-5ba1-a2f1-11880e1ccce4",
